<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Cook Islands Federation</h1><p class="st-meta">1893 CE – 1901 CE · capital Avarua</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A British protectorate uniting the chiefly governments of the Cook Islands under a federal parliament, before annexation by New Zealand in 1901.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Fearing French expansion from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tahiti">Tahiti</a>, the ariki of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-rarotonga">Rarotonga</a> and other islands accepted a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1888. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> politician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-moss">Frederick Moss</a>, appointed British Resident in 1891, persuaded the chiefs to form a federal government in the early 1890s, bringing the Kingdom of Rarotonga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aitutaki">Aitutaki</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangaia">Mangaia</a> and other islands under one parliament.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makea-takau-ariki">Makea Takau Ariki</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-rarotonga">Rarotonga</a> was chief of the federation, presiding over a federal council and parliament of ariki and elected members, while the islands kept their own councils. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-moss">Moss</a> pushed land and legal reforms but clashed with traders and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> interests, and he was replaced in 1898 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-gudgeon">Walter Gudgeon</a>. The economy relied on fruit and copra exports.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-seddon">Richard Seddon's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> government sought to expand in the Pacific. With the agreement of Makea and other ariki, the islands, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niue">Niue</a>, were annexed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> on 11 June 1901, becoming the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cook-islands">Cook Islands</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cook-islands-federation.jpg" alt="Cook Islands Federation" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday at English Wikipedia (Original text: Orange Tuesday (talk)) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Cook_Islands_Federation.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1893 CE – 1901 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Avarua</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Makea Takau Ariki, Frederick Moss</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cook Islands Māori, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cook-islands">Cook Islands</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Federation of the Cook Islands</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1897&amp;focus=cook-islands-federation" aria-label="Cook Islands Federation on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="386 2961 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cook Islands Federation lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M706 3217L706 3218M706 3217L4997 651M706 3217L706 3218"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M706 3218h0M4997 651h0M706 3218h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M706 3217h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M706 3217h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1897&amp;focus=cook-islands-federation">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
